/*  A book that pulls what it needs, so nobody else has to.

    This is the other half of bookkeeping.cufe. That module names `math` without pulling it and
    leaves the debt to whoever pulls the module — which is why ledger.cufe has to open
    `Pull a book on math.` around it. This file does the opposite: the book is written INSIDE the
    pull it needs, so it keeps it, and a program that wants pennies asks for pennies.

    ★ Both spellings are right, and the choice is about who the dependency belongs to. Defer, as
    bookkeeping does, when the caller is meant to supply it — a library that leaves `math` open can
    be handed a different one. Pull it yourself, as this does, when the dependency is an
    implementation detail and naming it at every call site would make it part of the spelling.

    ⚠ A module that pulls NOTHING has no provider of its own, so it still defers. That is not a
    fallback, it is the same rule read the other way: a name is satisfied where it is found.  */

Pull a book on math.
    /// Money, rounded to whole pennies.
    ///
    /// ★ It pulls `math` in its own file, so a program that wants pennies asks for pennies and
    /// nothing else. Compare bookkeeping.cufe, which leaves that dependency to its caller.
    Define object pennies with () and book:

        /// Rounds an amount to whole pennies, away from the halfway point.
        Bind number to to-the-penny, given (the number amount):
            Return (cast math's round on (amount * 100)) / 100.
        Done.

        /// Adds a series of amounts and rounds the result once, at the end.
        Bind number to total, given (the series of number amounts):
            Define running as 0.
            For each item in amounts, repeat:
                Increment running by item.
            Done.
            Return cast one's to-the-penny on (running).
        Done.
    Done.
Done.
